<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Appendix</h2>

<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1 </span>Faithfulness Metric</h3>

<div id="A1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate the faithfulness of explanations using a formal, composite metric. First, <span id="A1.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Strength of Evidence</span> confirms that the explanation cites input tokens with high model-internal saliency, as measured by gradient-based attribution. Second, <span id="A1.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Agreement with Truth</span> checks whether the explanation’s internal logic, when simulated, arrives at the correct conclusion.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In addition to this formal metric, our validation process includes a <span id="A1.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Relevance to Verbalisation</span> analysis. This step measures the semantic alignment between our mechanistic explanation and the model’s own Chain-of-Thought (CoT) output, where available. The purpose is not to treat the CoT as infallible ground truth, but rather to ensure our explanation does not fundamentally contradict the model’s own stated reasoning. This check serves as a guardrail against significant discrepancies. If our mechanistic explanation points to one reasoning path, but the model’s own stated logic describes an entirely different one, it suggests a potential flaw in either our explanation or the model’s self-awareness that warrants further investigation. The final Faithfulness Score (F) is computed as a weighted average of its normalised components: <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F=(w1\cdot E)+(w2\cdot A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F=(w1\cdot E)+(w2\cdot A)</annotation></semantics></math>, where E is the Strength of Evidence score derived from gradient-based attribution, A is the binary Agreement with Truth score, and the weights (w1,w2) are set to 0.5 for equal importance.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Our overall evaluation framework, combining the two-part metric with the Relevance to Verbalisation analysis, was validated to ensure its reliability, as detailed below. This ensures our findings are based on explanations that reliably reflect the model’s computational process. By combining evidence alignment, logical correctness, and verbalisation fidelity into a single composite score, we obtain a metric that ranges from completely unfaithful (when none of the criteria are met) to fully faithful (when all are satisfied). As a result, our metric provides a rigorous, interpretable measure for comparing explanation techniques and tracking progress toward genuinely transparent and trustworthy language models.</p>
</div>
